# Shell scripts Xcode Cloud runs must reach macOS with LF endings.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, which is the right default for Swift
# sources and a trap for `ci_scripts/`. A shebang line ending in CRLF is not
# `#!/bin/zsh` -- it is `#!/bin/zsh\r`, and the kernel looks for an interpreter
# named after the carriage return and fails with
#
#     /bin/zsh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which names the one file that is fine and points at nothing useful. The
# `-text` for the same reason: no end-of-line translation in either direction,
# so what is committed is byte-for-byte what Apple's runner executes.
#
# `.sh` under any directory rather than `ci_scripts/*.sh`, so a script added
# later somewhere else is covered without anyone remembering this file exists.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The XcodeGen spec is read by a tool that runs on macOS, and the generated
# project file is compared textually when a build goes wrong.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Property lists are read by macOS tooling -- the export options by
# `xcodebuild -exportArchive`, the entitlements by the linker -- and are
# generated on this machine with `core.autocrlf=true`. A CRLF plist is at best
# a noisy diff and at worst a plist that a strict parser rejects, and the
# failure names the setting rather than the line ending.
*.plist text eol=lf
*.entitlements text eol=lf

# Rendered images are large and must never be normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
